      "<p>Most Manhattan co-op closets are reach-ins, not walk-ins. A 24- to 30-inch-deep hallway box off the foyer, a shallow primary closet beside a radiator return, or a pair of flanking closets that share a bulkhead with the bathroom stack. Stock wire kits and franchise &ldquo;systems&rdquo; assume a clean rectangle. Prewar and postwar co-ops rarely give you one. Custom reach-in closet millwork starts from the measured opening &mdash; width, depth, ceiling height, and every pipe chase &mdash; so the hanging, drawers, and doors look intentional instead of jammed in.</p>",
      "<p>Green Cabinets NY designs and installs custom closet millwork by appointment in Manhattan, Brooklyn, and Queens. We are home-based in Bushwick &mdash; not a walk-in shop. Measure and sample visits happen in your apartment. For a Brooklyn-focused closet overview see our <a href=\"/blog/custom-closet-millwork-brooklyn\">custom closet millwork guide</a>. Kitchen work in the same buildings lives on <a href=\"/custom-kitchen-cabinets-manhattan\">Manhattan kitchen cabinets</a> and <a href=\"/blog/manhattan-condo-coop-kitchen-millwork\">condo/co-op kitchen millwork</a>.</p>",
      "<h2>Why Manhattan co-op reach-ins fight catalog closets</h2>",
      "<p>Co-op closets in Manhattan share a few patterns:</p>",
      "<ul>",
      "<li>Narrow depths (often under 26 inches clear) where a standard 24-inch hanging rod still needs clearance for hangers and door swing</li>",
      "<li>Out-of-square plaster walls and floors after decades of settlement</li>",
      "<li>Bulkheads, sprinkler heads, and soffits that cut the top of the opening</li>",
      "<li>Shared party walls with plumbing stacks that steal a corner of the footprint</li>",
      "<li>Double doors or bifolds that leave a dead zone in the middle when the kit is centered wrong</li>",
      "<li>Hall closets that must absorb coats, vacuum, and suitcase storage without becoming a pile</li>",
      "</ul>",
      "<p>A catalog kit drops in fixed uprights and fixed shelf spacing. Custom millwork scribes to the wall, sections tall units for freight, and puts drawers where your hands actually reach &mdash; usually the lower third &mdash; with double hanging or long hanging above.</p>",
      "<h2>Measure first: depth, door swing, and clear hanging</h2>",
      "<p>We measure the finished clear opening, not the nominal closet &ldquo;size&rdquo; on a floor plan. Typical Manhattan reach-ins need:</p>",
      "<ul>",
      "<li><strong>Depth</strong> that keeps hangers from hitting the door &mdash; often 22&ndash;28 inches clear after baseboard and drywall returns</li>",
      "<li><strong>Width</strong> that decides single vs double hanging, or a drawer stack on one side with hanging on the other</li>",
      "<li><strong>Height</strong> under bulkheads &mdash; full-height doors may need a short frieze or open shelf band so the millwork does not fight the soffit</li>",
      "<li><strong>Door type</strong> &mdash; swinging, bifold, or sliding &mdash; because drawer pulls and door hardware cannot collide</li>",
      "</ul>",
      "<p>Soft-close drawers keep early-morning noise down in thin-walled co-ops. Painted finishes and sealed veneers both show up often; humidity in hallway closets is usually milder than bathrooms, but we still seal edges properly. Door styles that read clean on a reach-in include <a href=\"/blog/shaker-vs-slim-shaker-nyc\">shaker and slim shaker</a>. Species and finish notes live in our <a href=\"/wood-species\">wood species guide</a> and <a href=\"/blog/wood-species-kitchen-cabinets-nyc\">wood species article</a>.</p>",
      "<h2>Co-op rules, COI, and quiet hours</h2>",
